      { "@type": "Question", name: "Should I price by the hour or by the inch of weld?", acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Hourly suits repair and one-off work. Production work is priced per inch of weld, per joint, or per pound of deposited metal. Start hourly with a not-to-exceed cap and move to per-joint pricing once you have data." } },
      { "@type": "Question", name: "How much deposit should a welder ask for?", acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "30-50% at signing is standard. Use 50% when the job requires buying steel stock or specialty filler upfront, so the deposit covers your material outlay." } },
      { "@type": "Question", name: "Why does TIG cost more than stick or MIG?", acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "TIG is slower, more skill-dependent, and needs both hands plus a foot pedal — but produces the cleanest, strongest welds on stainless and aluminum. A TIG hour produces fewer inches of weld than MIG, so the rate must be higher to earn the same." } },
      { "@type": "Question", name: "How much does it cost to weld a handrail?", acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "A typical steel handrail repair — re-weld a loose post or cracked joint, on-site — runs $150-$350. Most of it is the mobile call-out minimum plus 1-2 stick hours. A full custom indoor staircase rail fabrication runs $65-$125 per linear foot installed." } },
      { "@type": "Question", name: "How much is a mobile welder's minimum call-out?", acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "$150-$300 for the first hour or two is the national norm, with $1-$2 per loaded mile beyond a 20-30 mile radius. Roadside and emergency call-outs run 50-100% over shop rates. Quote the minimum before dispatching." } },
